---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Treadmill Motor Replacement Cost (2026): Parts, Labor, Worth It?";
const description =
  "A treadmill drive motor costs about $130 to $550 for most home brands (Amazon, Nov 2026); a technician adds about $185 to $270. Check the warranty first.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "By Price", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/price/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Motor Replacement Cost", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-motor-replacement-cost/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a treadmill motor cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On 25 November 2026, replacement drive motors for NordicTrack and ProForm treadmills listed at about $133 to $320 on Amazon and at Treadmill Doctor, with a 4.25 HP unit at $385.99. Sole, Spirit, Horizon and Bowflex motors mostly ran from about $300 to $550, and one Bowflex motor set was $904.99.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does it cost to replace a treadmill motor with labor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Add the technician's visit to the part. Treadmill Doctor's certified service was $269.99 for the first visit and one Pennsylvania-area repairer published $185 for diagnosis and labor (both checked 25 Nov 2026), so a fitted motor typically comes to about $320 to $820 on a home treadmill. Get written quotes, because labor varies by region and machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a treadmill motor covered by warranty?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Often, yes. Horizon, Sole and ProForm advertise lifetime motor warranties, and NordicTrack does on its Commercial and X models, though the T Series 10 has a 2-year motor term. Labor is usually covered for only one or two years, the cover is for the original purchaser, and repairs generally have to be authorised by the maker first.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is it worth replacing a treadmill motor?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is usually worth it on a mid-range treadmill with a sound frame, deck and belt, especially when the warranty pays for the motor. On a budget machine, a repair costing most of the price of a new one is rarely worth it. Compare the all-in quote with a comparable new treadmill before you decide.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I know if my treadmill motor or control board is bad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A burning smell from the motor housing, a very hot motor, or a gradual loss of power after the deck has been lubricated point to the motor. Sudden total failure, the wrong speed for the display, or error codes point to the board. They can fail together, so have both tested before buying either.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I replace a treadmill motor myself?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On most folding home treadmills, yes, if the machine is out of warranty and you are comfortable with basic tools: the motor is bolted in under the hood and connected by the drive belt and two wires. Unplug the machine first, match the part number exactly, and call a technician for any electrical fault such as a tripping breaker.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-25",
      dateModified: "2026-11-28",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/treadmill-motor-replacement-cost/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "price");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const GROUP_PAGES = GROUP?.pages ?? [];
const HERE_AT = GROUP_PAGES.findIndex((p) => p.url === HERE);
// The group's first 2 pages (its leads), then the pages after this one, wrapping round, so every page in a long
// hub group gets sibling links and the leads keep theirs (PLAYBOOK §0b L1).
const MORE = [...new Set([...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, 2), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(HERE_AT + 1), ...GROUP_PAGES.slice(0, Math.max(HERE_AT, 0))])].filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-25",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-28",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "treadmill motor replacement cost",
      "how much does a treadmill motor cost",
      "treadmill drive motor price",
      "replace treadmill motor",
      "treadmill repair cost",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Motor Prices", link: "#motor-prices" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/price/">By Price</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Price Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Treadmill Motor Replacement Cost: Parts, Labor and Whether It Pays</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        For most home treadmills the drive motor itself costs roughly $130 to $550, and a technician's visit adds about $185 to $270 on the published rates we found, so a fitted motor usually lands somewhere around $320 to $820. Before you pay anything, check the warranty: many brands cover the motor for life.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 28, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on retailer and maker listings, with prices checked on the dates shown.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#motor-prices" class="btn-ghost">Motor Prices by Brand</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          A failed treadmill motor sounds like the end of the machine, and sometimes it is. More often it
          is a part with a price you can look up, a labor charge you can get quoted, and a warranty that may
          already pay for the expensive half. This page lists what replacement drive motors were actually
          selling for on 25 November 2026, by brand and type, shows two repair companies' published visit
          rates, and then does the sum that matters: whether the total is worth spending on the treadmill
          you have.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="On this page">
          <h2>On this page</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#motor-prices">Treadmill Motor Prices by Brand</a></li>
            <li><a href="#by-type">Cost by Motor Type: Brushed DC, Brushless and AC</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty-first">Check the Warranty Before You Buy a Motor</a></li>
            <li><a href="#labor-cost">What a Technician Charges to Fit a Motor</a></li>
            <li><a href="#motor-or-not">Is It Really the Motor? Board, Brushes or Belt</a></li>
            <li><a href="#diy-vs-pro">DIY or Technician?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#repair-or-replace">Repair or Replace: The Decision Table</a></li>
            <li><a href="#questions-to-ask">What to Ask a Repair Shop</a></li>
            <li><a href="#buying-a-motor">Buying a Replacement Motor Yourself</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Replacing a home treadmill's drive motor typically costs about $130 to $550 for the part
            and roughly $320 to $820 with a technician, but a lifetime motor warranty may cover the part
            entirely, so check that before anything else.</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>NordicTrack and ProForm motors</strong> (iFIT machines) were the cheapest to buy:
            aftermarket 2.8 HP and 3.0 HP replacements listed at about $133 to $230 on Amazon, and Treadmill
            Doctor listed many iFIT motors at $199.99 to $319.99 (all 25 Nov 2026).</li>
            <li><strong>Sole, Spirit, Horizon and Bowflex motors</strong> cost more: about $324 to $550 for
            most listings we found, and up to $904.99 for one Bowflex motor set.</li>
            <li><strong>Labor:</strong> Treadmill Doctor's certified-technician service was $269.99 for the
            first visit, and one Pennsylvania-area repairer published $185 for diagnosis and labor, parts
            extra (both checked 25 Nov 2026).</li>
            <li><strong>Warranty:</strong> Horizon, Sole and ProForm advertise lifetime motor cover, and
            NordicTrack does on its Commercial and X models, but labor is usually covered for only one or
            two years and the cover is for the original buyer.</li>
            <li><strong>Rule out the cheap faults first:</strong> worn brushes, a slipping drive belt, a dry
            deck or a failed control board produce the same symptoms for far less money.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For how long a motor should last in the first place, and what wears one out, see our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmill motors last</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-prices" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Treadmill Motor Prices by Brand</h2>
<p>
            The table below lists replacement drive motors we found on sale on 25 November 2026, on Amazon and
            at Treadmill Doctor, a long-running US <a href="/treadmill-parts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>treadmill parts</a> seller. Every price is the listing price on
            that day, before tax and shipping. Motors are sold by part number, not by brand, so the right
            motor for your machine depends on the model and serial number on its frame decal. Treat the rows
            as a guide to what each brand's motors cost, not as a fit list.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Brand or machine</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Motor listed</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Price (25 Nov 2026)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Where</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack and ProForm, many folding models</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Aftermarket 2.8 HP 130 V DC with flywheel (part M-405565 and equivalents)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$168.00-$195.99 across seven listings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack C 990, ProForm Performance 800i and others</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Aftermarket 3.0 HP 130 V DC with flywheel (part M-405560)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$133.20-$229.99 across five listings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Older ProForm, Weslo and Gold's Gym models</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill Doctor replacement motors (parts 405692, 349568, 248147 and others)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$199.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill Doctor</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack Commercial 1750 (by model number)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor, parts 287483, 313692, 328330, 405694</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$269.99-$319.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill Doctor</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack 1750, 2450, X22i; ProForm Pro series</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4.25 HP 130 V DC (parts 321628 or 405694)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$385.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon (Hydra Fitness Exchange)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole (F-series)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole drive motor CRG080601A-01 or CRG080603A</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$323.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill Doctor</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole F60, F63, F80, F85</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Model-specific DC motors with flywheel</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$370.99-$429.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon (Hydra Fitness Exchange)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Spirit and Xterra</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC motors for XT175, XT285, Xterra T6.3</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$407.99-$550.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon 7.0 AT and T5</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.0 HP DC motor (JM01-015) and T5 motor (JM12-001), sold as tested</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$389.99-$413.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Older Horizon (T95, DT680)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.75 HP drive motor set 016172-Z</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$377.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill Doctor</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bowflex</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor 1000512080; TreadClimber motor 1000514190</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$329.99; $299.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill Doctor</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bowflex (T25i, T56i, Treadmill 22)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC motor sets with flywheel</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$451.99-$904.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon (Hydra Fitness Exchange)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Precor home and club models</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DC drive motors, sold as tested</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$293.99-$719.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Life Fitness Activate series</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">AC drive motor OK75, sold as tested</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$419.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Amazon</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Matrix TF30</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor 1000386694</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$791.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill Doctor</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Three patterns stand out. <strong>iFIT machines are the cheapest to re-motor</strong>, because
            NordicTrack, ProForm, Weslo and Gold's Gym share a small family of 130-volt motors and several
            aftermarket sellers make copies of them. <strong>Sole, Spirit, Horizon and Bowflex motors cost
            roughly twice as much</strong>, with fewer sellers and, in our searches, almost no low-cost copies. <strong>Commercial brands vary the most</strong>: a Precor motor ran from $293.99 to $719.99
            depending on the model. Most of those motors go into used club machines, since <a href="/precor-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor</a> now sells treadmills only to commercial buyers. A whole remanufactured Precor, by comparison, ran $2,495 to $4,799 in late November, per our <a href="/how-much-does-a-precor-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor treadmill prices</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Read the condition before you buy. Several of the Horizon, Precor and Life Fitness listings above
            describe the motor only as &ldquo;Tested and 100% working&rdquo; and charge a 25% restocking fee on
            returns, which suggests a used or pulled part rather than a new one; ask the seller if it matters
            to you. One of the cheapest aftermarket iFIT motors states a one-year quality assurance on its
            listing, and most of the rest do not state a warranty at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Cost by Motor Type: Brushed DC, Brushless and AC</h2>
<p>
            The kind of motor in your treadmill changes both the price of a replacement and how likely you are
            to need one. Our guide to <a href="/ac-vs-dc-treadmill-motor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">AC vs DC treadmill motors</a> explains the engineering; here is what it means for the bill.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Motor type</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Where you find it</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Replacement motor prices we saw (25 Nov 2026)</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Cheaper fix to try first</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Brushed DC, iFIT family</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack, ProForm, Weslo, Gold's Gym</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $133-$320; $385.99 for the 4.25 HP unit</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Brushes: $39.95-$49.95 for a ProForm kit on Amazon</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Brushed DC, other home brands</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole, Spirit, Horizon, Bowflex</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $299-$551, one Bowflex set $904.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Brushes, drive belt, deck lubrication</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Brushless DC</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking pads and some newer folding treadmills, such as the Sole F50</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Rarely sold separately; one walking-pad motor listed at $59.99</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">No brushes to replace; check the controller and belt</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">AC</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Commercial and light-commercial machines</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$419.99 for a Life Fitness Activate motor, sold as tested</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The inverter (drive) is a more common failure than the motor</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Brushed DC motors</strong> are in most home treadmills. They are the only type with a
            routine wear part, the carbon brushes, and a motor that has lost power or started to crackle often
            needs nothing more than a new pair. Generic brush pairs listed from $8.99 on Amazon on 25 November
            2026, ProForm brush kits at $39.95 to $49.95, and a kit for Precor and Life Fitness DC
            motors at $84.75 to $89.90. Our <a href="/treadmill-motor-brushes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill motor brushes</a> guide shows how to check them. Brushes cannot help a motor with a burnt winding or a badly scored
            commutator.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Brushless motors</strong> have no brushes to wear, which is a real advantage, but their
            electronics are matched to the motor, and walking-pad makers seldom sell either part to owners.
            We found very few brushless replacement motors on Amazon, and the one walking-pad motor we found
            (listed for a &ldquo;V300&rdquo; pad at $59.99) came from a seller with no obvious link to any
            treadmill brand. On a walking pad, ask the maker's support team first; if they cannot supply a
            motor, the pad's price usually makes replacement the sensible answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>AC motors</strong> are the most durable of the three and tend to outlast the electronics
            that drive them. The motor itself is not dramatically more expensive than a large DC motor, but AC
            treadmills are usually heavy commercial machines, so labor, not the part, tends to set the bill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Check the Warranty Before You Buy a Motor</h2>
<p>
            Lifetime motor warranties are common on mid-range home treadmills, so the most expensive part of
            this repair is often already paid for. What usually is not covered is the labor after the first
            year or two, and that changes the sum.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Brand</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Motor cover stated</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Labor cover stated</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Source</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon (T101, T202, 7.0 AT, 7.4 AT, 7.8 AT)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime frame and motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year (T101, T202, 7.0 AT); 2 years (7.4 AT, 7.8 AT)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon, as recorded in our Horizon price guide, Nov 2026; the T101 Connect's Amazon title also states a lifetime frame and motor warranty (25 Nov 2026)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime frame and motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not stated on the product pages we checked</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole product pages, Nov 2026</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">ProForm</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime motor on the current range</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Parts and labor options of 1, 3 or 5 years; older manuals give 1 year</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">ProForm website and manuals, Nov 2026</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack X24</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive motor for the lifetime of the original purchaser</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">X24 user's manual, model NTL39225.0, Nov 2026</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack Commercial 1750, 2450, X16</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime motor advertised</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the manual for your model</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack product pages, Nov 2026</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack T Series 10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2-year motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Check the manual for your model</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack product page, Nov 2026</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Four conditions decide whether that cover helps you:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Original purchaser only.</strong> The NordicTrack and ProForm manuals we read state
            that the warranty is not transferable, so a used treadmill normally comes without the lifetime
            motor.</li>
            <li><strong>Pre-authorisation.</strong> iFIT requires warranty repairs to be approved and carried
            out through its authorised providers. A motor you buy on Amazon and fit yourself is not a warranty
            claim, and opening up an in-warranty machine before calling can give the maker a reason to
            refuse.</li>
            <li><strong>Labor and trip charges.</strong> After the labor term ends, you may get a free motor
            and still pay for someone to fit it. The iFIT manuals also mention a possible handling charge for
            shipped parts and a trip charge for in-home service, without stating amounts, so ask when you book.</li>
            <li><strong>Registration and paperwork.</strong> Have the model and serial number from the frame
            decal and proof of purchase ready. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> covers how to make a claim with each major brand.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If the motor is covered but labor is not, the realistic cost of the repair is the technician's
            visit, which on the rates below is roughly $185 to $270, plus any handling or trip charge the maker
            applies. That is a very different decision from paying $400 for a part and $270 to fit it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="labor-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What a Technician Charges to Fit a Motor</h2>
<p>
            Few repair companies publish their prices, and labor varies with region, travel distance and how
            hard your machine is to work on. We found two US services with published rates on 25 November 2026.
            They are examples, not averages; get at least two written quotes for your own machine.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Service</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Published price</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it covers</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill Doctor certified service (nationwide referral)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$269.99 first visit; $229.99 each additional visit</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A certified technician arranged by Treadmill Doctor fits parts you order from it; parts are extra</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">treadmillfixation.com (Pennsylvania-area repairer), repair visit</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$185.00 plus parts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Diagnosis, labor and installation of non-warranty parts</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">treadmillfixation.com, diagnostic only</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$125.00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Diagnosis; a return visit to fit customer-ordered or warranty parts is $130.00</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">treadmillfixation.com, warranty parts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$255.00 plus parts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Diagnosis, labor and a return visit to install warranty replacement parts</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Several things push a labor quote up or down:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Diagnosis versus fitting.</strong> A technician who has to find the fault first, then
            order the part, then come back, charges for two visits. Knowing the part number in advance saves
            money only if your diagnosis is right.</li>
            <li><strong>Machine type.</strong> On most folding home treadmills the motor sits under a hood
            that comes off with a few screws. Heavy commercial machines, incline trainers and treadmills with
            the motor under a large console take longer.</li>
            <li><strong>Extra parts found on the day.</strong> A worn drive belt, a scorched control board or a
            dry deck that caused the failure can add parts and time.</li>
            <li><strong>Travel and region.</strong> Rural call-outs and high-cost cities both raise the visit
            charge. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-repair-near-me/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">finding a treadmill repair technician</a> covers how to find a qualified one.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Put together, an iFIT motor at about $200 fitted on a single $185 to $270 visit comes to roughly
            $385 to $470. A Sole motor at $323.99 with the same visit comes to roughly $510 to $595, and a
            $450 to $550 Bowflex or Spirit motor lands at roughly $640 to $820, before tax and shipping. A
            diagnostic visit and a second visit to fit the part add another $125 to $230 on the rates above. Set that against a new T6, which was $899 on Bowflex’s store in its 24 November 2026 sale, per our <a href="/how-much-does-a-bowflex-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex treadmill prices</a> page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-or-not" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is It Really the Motor? Board, Brushes or Belt</h2>
<p>
            Motors are rarely the first part to fail, and the parts that share their symptoms are much cheaper.
            The motor control board is the usual suspect: on 25 November 2026 an ICON board (MC1618DLS) for
            NordicTrack and ProForm listed at $198.99 on Amazon, Treadmill Doctor's MC-2100 board at $199.99,
            aftermarket <a href="/sole-f63-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Sole F63</a> boards at $128.89 to $152.99 on Amazon, Treadmill Doctor's Sole board at
            $296.99, and Horizon boards at $179.00 to $359.99 on Amazon. A board can cost as much as a motor,
            so guessing wrong is expensive either way.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Symptom</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Points toward</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Check first</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console lights, belt does not move, no motor sound</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Control board, safety key, wiring, then motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety key; board fuse and indicator lights; motor connector</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor spins, belt does not</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Drive belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hood off: does the pulley turn the front roller?</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt slows when you step on it</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dry deck or loose belt before the motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubricate and adjust; retest before buying parts</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Crackling, loss of power, ozone smell</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Worn brushes or a scored commutator</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remove the brush caps and measure the brushes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Burning smell from the motor housing, motor very hot</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor windings</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop using it; technician with a meter</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed wrong for the display, or sudden total failure</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Control board or speed sensor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Error codes; board indicator light; sensor alignment</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Breaker trips at start</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor short, board or wiring</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stop resetting it; have it tested</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Two of the cheapest checks clear up a lot of supposed motor failures. A belt that slows under your
            feet is usually friction from a dry deck, which is why our <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to a belt that slows when you step on it</a> starts with lubrication. And a treadmill that lights up but will not move is more often a board,
            key or drive-belt fault than a dead motor; our <a href="/treadmill-turns-on-but-belt-wont-move/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill turns on but the belt won't move</a> guide walks through the order.
          </p>
          <p>
            Even the sellers of aftermarket motors warn about this. Two of the NordicTrack and ProForm motor
            listings we read advise checking the motor control board before buying a motor, because an
            incompatible or faulty board can stop a new motor working or damage it. The reverse is also true:
            a motor dragging a dry belt for months can overheat a board. If both are suspect, have them tested
            together rather than replacing one and hoping.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-vs-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>DIY or Technician?</h2>
<p>
            On most folding home treadmills, swapping a drive motor is a bolt-on job: hood off, drive belt off,
            motor wires disconnected, mounting bolts out, new motor in, belt tension reset. Owners who are
            comfortable with a socket set and a multimeter do it. The savings are real: on the published rates
            above, doing it yourself saves roughly $185 to $270.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>DIY makes sense when</strong> the machine is out of warranty, the fault has been narrowed to
            the motor (a technician's diagnostic visit at about $125 can be money well spent here), you can
            match the part number on the old motor to the new one, and the treadmill is a common home model
            with an accessible motor bay.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Call a technician when</strong> any part of the warranty might apply; the machine trips the
            breaker or smells of burning, which points to an electrical fault that needs testing; the board and
            motor are both suspect; or the treadmill is a heavy commercial machine. Pay attention to safety too:
            unplug the treadmill and wait before opening the hood, because control boards can hold a charge,
            and read the service warnings in your owner's manual before you start.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever route you take, budget for the small extras that go with the job. Treadmill Doctor lists
            a lubricant it recommends when replacing a motor at $11.99, and a new drive belt is worth fitting
            while the old one is off if it is glazed or cracked; our <a href="/treadmill-drive-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill drive belt</a> guide shows how to tell. If the deck is dry or the walking belt is worn, fix that too, or the new
            motor will work against the same friction that may have killed the old one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="repair-or-replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Repair or Replace: The Decision Table</h2>
<p>
            The right answer depends less on the motor than on the treadmill around it. The table compares a
            typical out-of-warranty motor repair with what a comparable new machine costs today. New prices are
            the dated figures from our own price guides; the repair figures use the motor prices and labor
            rates above.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Your treadmill</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">New price today</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Motor repair, DIY / with technician</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Usual call</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">ProForm Carbon TL</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$479 (Amazon, 25 Nov 2026)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About $133-$230 / $320-$500</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">DIY if the frame and belt are good; otherwise replace</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon T202</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$799.99 (Amazon, 25 Nov 2026)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime motor warranty for the original owner; otherwise Horizon motors we found were $377.99-$413.99, or about $565-$685 fitted</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Claim the warranty; out of it, borderline</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon 7.0 AT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$934.98 (Amazon, 25 Nov 2026)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty; otherwise about $414 / $600-$685</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Claim the warranty; out of it, repair if under about 8 years old</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack T Series 10</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$949.05 (Amazon, 25 Nov 2026)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2-year motor cover; after that about $133-$230 / $320-$500</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair: iFIT motors are cheap</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole F63</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,099.99 (Amazon, 25 Nov 2026)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty; otherwise about $324-$430 / $510-$700</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Claim the warranty; out of it, usually repair</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack Commercial 1750</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,999 (Amazon, 25 Nov 2026)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty; otherwise about $270-$386 / $455-$655</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Repair</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            A rule of thumb consistent with our <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill repair guide</a>:
            repair when the all-in cost is well under half the price of a comparable new machine, the frame,
            deck and walking belt are sound, and the treadmill is not near the end of its expected life.
            Replace when the repair is most of the price of a new one, when other parts are also worn, or when
            a budget machine has failed from being used harder than it was built for. A new motor in a
            treadmill with a <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>worn belt</a> and a dry deck is often followed by a new board or belt a few months
            later.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also weigh what you would get instead. A replacement brings a fresh warranty, including a lifetime
            motor on many models, and current features. A repaired mid-range machine with a heavy frame may
            still be a better treadmill than a new budget one at the same money. If you decide to replace, our <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill price guide</a> shows what each tier costs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="questions-to-ask" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Ask a Repair Shop</h2>
<p>
            A clear quote protects you from paying twice. Ask these before you agree to a motor replacement:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>How did you confirm it is the motor?</strong> Ask whether the board, brushes, drive
            belt and speed sensor were tested, and how.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the price itemised?</strong> Visit or diagnostic fee, labor, the motor, any other
            parts, shipping and tax, each on its own line.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the diagnostic fee credited if I go ahead?</strong> Some technicians apply it to the
            repair; others charge it separately.</li>
            <li><strong>Is the motor new, remanufactured or used, and is it the maker's part or an aftermarket
            one?</strong> Ask for the part number.</li>
            <li><strong>What warranty comes with the part and with the labor?</strong> Get it in writing,
            including what happens if the motor fails again within that period.</li>
            <li><strong>Will a second visit be needed?</strong> If the part has to be ordered, ask what the
            return visit costs.</li>
            <li><strong>Are you authorised by my treadmill's maker?</strong> This matters if any part of the
            warranty still applies, because an unauthorised repair may not be reimbursed.</li>
            <li><strong>What else did you see?</strong> A technician with the hood off can tell you about a <a href="/treadmill-deck-replacement/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>worn
            deck</a>, belt or rollers, which is information you need before deciding to repair.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            A technician who will not explain how the fault was diagnosed, or who quotes only a single total
            with no breakdown, is worth a second opinion before you pay for a motor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-a-motor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a Replacement Motor Yourself</h2>
<p>
            If you are buying the part, the details on the old motor matter more than the brand on the frame.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Start with the model and serial number</strong> on the frame decal. Parts sellers,
            including Treadmill Doctor and the Hydra Fitness Exchange listings on Amazon, look up motors by
            model number, and several iFIT motors differ between versions of the same treadmill. The
            NordicTrack Commercial 1750, for example, used at least four different motor part numbers across
            its model numbers in Treadmill Doctor's listings.</li>
            <li><strong>Match the old motor's label</strong>: part number, voltage, rated horsepower and whether
            the flywheel and pulley are included. Many listings sell the motor with the flywheel attached, but
            not all.</li>
            <li><strong>Check that the board suits the motor.</strong> A 90-volt motor on a 130-volt board, or
            the reverse, will not run properly.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the return terms.</strong> Some listings charge a 25% restocking fee, which makes
            a wrong guess costly.</li>
            <li><strong>Treat horsepower claims with care.</strong> Replacement listings quote treadmill-duty
            and continuous-duty figures that do not always match the original spec sheet. Fit the part
            number, not the bigger number; our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a> explains why ratings differ.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We could not read prices on the manufacturers' own parts stores on 25 November 2026: Sole's and
            Horizon's sites blocked automated requests or required a model to be selected, and NordicTrack's and
            ProForm's parts pages did not load. If your treadmill is a current model, ask the maker's support
            line for a quote too; original parts sometimes cost more but match exactly.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Mistakes to Avoid With a Motor Replacement</h2>
          <p>Most overspending on treadmill motors comes from buying a part before the fault, or the warranty, has been checked.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a motor before checking the warranty</h3>
              <p>A motor you buy and fit yourself is not a warranty claim, and opening an in-warranty machine first can complicate one. Read the manual and call the maker with the serial number before ordering anything.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Replacing the motor when the board failed</h3>
              <p>Control boards fail with the same symptoms and cost about the same. Have the board, brushes and drive belt checked first, or pay for a diagnostic visit, rather than guessing with a $200 to $400 part.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Fitting a new motor to a dry deck</h3>
              <p>Friction from a dry deck or worn belt makes the motor work harder and can kill a new one. Lubricate the deck, and replace a worn walking or drive belt, in the same job.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ordering by brand instead of part number</h3>
              <p>One model name can use several different motors across its versions. Match the part number, voltage and flywheel on the old motor, and check the seller's return terms before ordering.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">A treadmill motor is usually a $130 to $550 part</strong> on a home
            machine, with NordicTrack and ProForm motors at the cheap end and Sole, Spirit, Horizon and Bowflex
            motors costing more (prices checked 25 November 2026). A technician's visit adds roughly $185 to
            $270 on the published rates we found, so a fitted motor typically comes to about $320 to $820.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Check the warranty first and the diagnosis second.</strong> Many brands
            cover the motor for life for the original owner, and brushes, belts and control boards cause the same
            symptoms for less. If the motor really has failed and the warranty will not pay, repair a sound
            mid-range machine and replace a worn budget one. Our <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill repair guide</a> covers the other repairs worth knowing about.
          </p>
        </div>

