        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>How much does a treadmill motor cost?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On 25 November 2026, replacement drive motors for NordicTrack and ProForm treadmills listed at about $133 to $320 on Amazon and at Treadmill Doctor, with a 4.25 HP unit at $385.99. Sole, Spirit, Horizon and Bowflex motors mostly ran from about $300 to $550, and one Bowflex motor set was $904.99.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does it cost to replace a treadmill motor with labor?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Add the technician's visit to the part. Treadmill Doctor's certified service was $269.99 for the first visit and one Pennsylvania-area repairer published $185 for diagnosis and labor (both checked 25 Nov 2026), so a fitted motor typically comes to about $320 to $820 on a home treadmill. Get written quotes, because labor varies by region and machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a treadmill motor covered by warranty?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often, yes. Horizon, Sole and ProForm advertise lifetime motor warranties, and NordicTrack does on its Commercial and X models, though the T Series 10 has a 2-year motor term. Labor is usually covered for only one or two years, the cover is for the original purchaser, and repairs generally have to be authorised by the maker first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is it worth replacing a treadmill motor?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is usually worth it on a mid-range treadmill with a sound frame, deck and belt, especially when the warranty pays for the motor. On a budget machine, a repair costing most of the price of a new one is rarely worth it. Compare the all-in quote with a comparable new treadmill before you decide.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I know if my treadmill motor or control board is bad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A burning smell from the motor housing, a very hot motor, or a gradual loss of power after the deck has been lubricated point to the motor. Sudden total failure, the wrong speed for the display, or error codes point to the board. They can fail together, so have both tested before buying either.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I replace a treadmill motor myself?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On most folding home treadmills, yes, if the machine is out of warranty and you are comfortable with basic tools: the motor is bolted in under the hood and connected by the drive belt and two wires. Unplug the machine first, match the part number exactly, and call a technician for any electrical fault such as a tripping breaker.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

